WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:20.313
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. The patterns in popular software.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.402
Every pattern in the catalog, shown in software you have used.

3
00:00:36.782 --> 00:00:47.335
The examples are chosen on one rule: the company or project wrote the decision down — in an engineering blog, a paper, a talk, or a repository — so the claim can be checked.

4
00:00:47.715 --> 00:00:50.687
And every stop has the same four beats.

5
00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:52.633
The problem.

6
00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:54.625
The pattern.

7
00:00:55.665 --> 00:00:57.047
The named case.

8
00:00:58.087 --> 00:01:02.255
And the cost — because a pattern with no cost is a slogan.

9
00:01:03.235 --> 00:01:16.865
Shopify runs one of the largest Ruby on Rails codebases in existence and kept it a monolith — a modular monolith, with a tool called Packwerk that fails the build when one component reaches into another’s internals.

10
00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:22.632
Stack Overflow serves hundreds of millions of page views a month from a single .NET application.

11
00:01:23.012 --> 00:01:38.523
And two documented returns: in 2023 Amazon Prime Video moved a serverless monitoring pipeline into one process and cut its infrastructure cost by about ninety percent; in 2018 Segment folded about a hundred and forty services back into one.

12
00:01:39.223 --> 00:01:50.531
On the other side, Amazon, Netflix, Uber with about two thousand two hundred critical microservices, and Monzo, a bank built on about fifteen hundred services in Go.

13
00:01:50.911 --> 00:01:53.964
The cost of the second choice is the rest of this episode.

14
00:01:54.944 --> 00:02:02.572
By capability: Amazon’s service teams organized around what a retailer does; Netflix’s around what a streaming business does.

15
00:02:02.952 --> 00:02:13.680
By subdomain: Uber, in 2020, put a domain layer above its two thousand two hundred services — domain-oriented microservice architecture, one gateway per domain, dependencies pointing downward.

16
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:20.457
Service per team: Amazon’s two-pizza teams, Spotify’s squads, Netflix’s full-cycle developers.

17
00:02:21.157 --> 00:02:30.688
And the self-contained service: Netflix’s personalization runs offline, nearline, and online, so the homepage is answered from data already at hand.

18
00:02:31.668 --> 00:02:33.387
The strangler application.

19
00:02:34.427 --> 00:02:43.552
Netflix took from 2009 to January 2016 to strangle its data center — non-member systems first, billing last.

20
00:02:43.932 --> 00:02:55.821
Airbnb strangled its Rails monolith, Monorail, from 2018: reads before writes, one endpoint at a time, old and new paths compared side by side with tools like GitHub’s Scientist and Twitter’s Diffy.

21
00:02:56.521 --> 00:03:15.503
The anti-corruption layer: Airbnb’s data services wrapped the monolith’s tables behind Thrift interfaces; and Amazon’s consumer business turned off its last Oracle database in October 2019 — about seventy-five hundred databases moved team by team, because since the mandate nobody had read another team’s tables directly.

22
00:03:16.483 --> 00:03:17.725
The biggest stop.

23
00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:26.393
Database per service: Amazon’s ‘no direct database reads’; Monzo, where each service owns its own Cassandra keyspace.

24
00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:33.704
The shared database, kept in the language as a real choice — Shopify’s processes share the shard’s MySQL on purpose.

25
00:03:34.404 --> 00:03:50.205
The saga: Microsoft’s eShop reference application places an order across Ordering, Basket, Catalog, and Payment, and its compensation is to move the order to Cancelled; Uber built Cadence and Netflix built Conductor to run long sagas durably.

26
00:03:50.585 --> 00:03:57.493
Choreography is eShop’s chain of integration events, and LinkedIn’s Kafka carrying that style at company scale.

27
00:03:57.873 --> 00:04:03.365
Orchestration is Conductor, Cadence and its fork Temporal, and AWS Step Functions.

28
00:04:04.065 --> 00:04:13.527
The command-side replica: eShop’s Basket keeps catalog prices updated by events; Netflix’s EVCache replicates data across regions.

29
00:04:13.907 --> 00:04:24.727
API composition: Amazon’s product page drawing on more than a hundred services; Netflix moving that composition to GraphQL Federation in 2020.

30
00:04:25.107 --> 00:04:31.400
CQRS: Twitter’s ‘Timelines at Scale’, 2012 — fan out on write, one Redis fetch on read.

31
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:40.227
Domain events: Stripe’s event feed and Shopify’s webhooks, sold as products; Zalando’s Nakadi with a schema registry inside the company.

32
00:04:40.607 --> 00:04:49.489
Event sourcing: the LMAX exchange, journaling every input, and Netflix’s Downloads license service, candid about schema evolution.

33
00:04:50.469 --> 00:04:56.436
Write the order, publish OrderPlaced, and crash in between — the order exists and nobody knows.

34
00:04:56.816 --> 00:05:07.962
The transactional outbox: eShop writes an integration-event row in the same transaction and publishes it afterwards; Debezium’s outbox router, 2019, made that infrastructure.

35
00:05:08.342 --> 00:05:19.708
Transaction log tailing: LinkedIn’s Databus in 2012 is the ancestor; Debezium, Airbnb’s SpinalTap, Netflix’s DBLog, Facebook’s Wormhole are the descendants.

36
00:05:20.408 --> 00:05:28.663
The polling publisher: the simplest thing — Richardson’s Eventuate Tram, and Stripe retrying webhook deliveries with backoff for up to three days.

37
00:05:29.643 --> 00:05:48.706
Remote procedure invocation: Google’s internal Stubby became gRPC in 2015; Facebook built Thrift in 2007 so PHP, C++, Java, and Python could call each other; Twitter’s Finagle made a service a function from request to future response.

38
00:05:49.086 --> 00:06:00.894
Messaging: Kafka, built at LinkedIn in 2010 and open-sourced in 2011; and Amazon SQS, in beta in 2004 and generally available in 2006 — before EC2.

39
00:06:01.274 --> 00:06:04.733
Amazon shipped a queue before it shipped a computer.

40
00:06:05.433 --> 00:06:13.653
Domain-specific protocols: Facebook Messenger over MQTT, WhatsApp over a compressed XMPP, Discord’s gateway over WebSockets.

41
00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:22.427
And the idempotent consumer: Stripe’s idempotency keys, described by Brandur Leach in 2017 — a retry that cannot charge twice.

42
00:06:23.407 --> 00:06:40.486
The API gateway: Netflix’s Zuul, open-sourced in 2013 and rebuilt on Netty as Zuul 2 in 2018; Amazon API Gateway and Kong in 2015; Uber’s edge gateway exposing thousands of endpoints from configuration.

43
00:06:40.866 --> 00:06:55.274
The backend for frontend: Phil Calçado’s SoundCloud practice, written up by Sam Newman in 2015; Netflix’s device teams writing their own adapter scripts inside the API tier; eShop’s web and mobile aggregators behind Envoy.

44
00:06:55.654 --> 00:07:05.464
The rule holds only while one experience has one backend — a backend shared by two clients is a gateway again.

45
00:07:06.784 --> 00:07:21.482
Client-side discovery: Netflix’s Eureka and Ribbon, 2012 — the client caches the registry, so a Eureka outage does not stop calls; availability chosen over consistency.

46
00:07:22.522 --> 00:07:36.454
Server-side discovery: a Kubernetes Service, a stable name forwarded to a ready pod by kernel rules; AWS load balancers from 2009; Airbnb’s SmartStack with a local HAProxy on every host.

47
00:07:36.834 --> 00:07:46.308
The registry itself: Eureka, ZooKeeper, HashiCorp Consul, and etcd — which makes etcd the registry of every Kubernetes cluster without anyone calling it that.

48
00:07:47.008 --> 00:08:00.406
Self registration is the Eureka client and its thirty-second heartbeat; third-party registration is Kubernetes writing pod addresses into EndpointSlices, and Netflix’s Prana sidecar registering non-Java processes.

49
00:08:01.386 --> 00:08:06.947
Michael Nygard named these in ‘Release It!’, 2007, and Netflix turned them into a library.

50
00:08:07.327 --> 00:08:23.918
Hystrix, 2012: the circuit opens when at least twenty requests in a ten-second window fail more than half the time, stays open for five seconds, then tries one call; every command has a fallback, so the homepage lost a row, not the page.

51
00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:30.507
It entered maintenance in 2018, and Envoy’s outlier detection now does the job in the proxy.

52
00:08:31.207 --> 00:08:37.628
Timeout: gRPC’s deadlines propagate down the chain, so the third service knows how much time is left.

53
00:08:38.008 --> 00:08:49.049
Retry: Marc Brooker’s 2015 ‘Exponential Backoff and Jitter’, and Google’s retry budgets — stop retrying when retries exceed ten percent of requests.

54
00:08:49.429 --> 00:08:59.645
Bulkhead: Hystrix’s per-dependency thread pools, Shopify’s Semian tickets, and AWS cells — compartments, so one flooding dependency cannot sink the ship.

55
00:09:00.625 --> 00:09:02.065
The access token.

56
00:09:03.105 --> 00:09:18.639
OAuth 2.0 standardized how a client obtains one, in 2012; JSON Web Token, in 2015, standardized a signed token a service can verify without a network call. eShop’s Identity service issues them and Ordering checks the signature locally.

57
00:09:19.019 --> 00:09:30.838
Netflix’s Passport, described in 2021, terminates every kind of device authentication at the edge and mints one short-lived token that travels to every downstream service.

58
00:09:31.538 --> 00:09:36.879
And SPIFFE certificates carry the other half: which service is calling, as opposed to on whose behalf.

59
00:09:37.859 --> 00:09:40.982
A request is now a path across processes.

60
00:09:42.022 --> 00:09:54.270
Log aggregation: the ELK stack, Fluentd on every Kubernetes node, and Grafana Loki, which indexes only labels because full-text indexing had become the largest line item.

61
00:09:55.310 --> 00:10:05.446
Metrics: Etsy’s StatsD and ‘Measure Anything, Measure Everything’, 2011; Prometheus, born at SoundCloud, the second project accepted by the CNCF.

62
00:10:06.146 --> 00:10:12.531
Audit logging: AWS CloudTrail recording every API call in an account since 2013.

63
00:10:12.911 --> 00:10:27.052
Distributed tracing: Google’s Dapper paper in 2010, Twitter’s Zipkin, Uber’s Jaeger, OpenTelemetry in 2019, and Netflix’s Edgar assembling a whole playback session for support.

64
00:10:27.432 --> 00:10:29.673
Exception tracking: Sentry.

65
00:10:30.713 --> 00:10:34.184
Health checks: Kubernetes probes that separate ‘alive’ from ‘ready’.

66
00:10:34.564 --> 00:10:39.800
And logging deployments: Etsy’s Deployinator drew a line on every graph at every deploy.

67
00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:44.600
The unit that starts and scales changed three times in twenty years.

68
00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:54.616
Several services on one application server, then one service per machine — Google’s 2003 search cluster, one role per host.

69
00:10:54.996 --> 00:11:06.711
Then one service per virtual machine: Netflix baking every service into its own machine image from 2011, red-black deploys, never patching a running instance.

70
00:11:07.751 --> 00:11:19.268
Then one service per container: Google’s Borg had run everything in cgroups for a decade before the 2015 paper; Docker made the image portable; Kubernetes made the scheduler a commodity.

71
00:11:19.648 --> 00:11:32.245
Then the function: AWS Lambda, 2014 — and the boundary of the pattern, Prime Video’s 2023 return to one long-running process.

72
00:11:33.285 --> 00:11:39.821
Over all of it, the deployment platform: Heroku’s git push, Kubernetes, and Netflix’s Spinnaker and Titus.

73
00:11:40.801 --> 00:11:43.030
Library first, then proxy.

74
00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:50.363
The chassis: Dropwizard in 2011, Spring Boot and Spring Cloud Netflix, Monzo’s Typhon.

75
00:11:50.743 --> 00:12:01.622
Externalized configuration: the Twelve-Factor rule, Netflix’s Archaius tuning Hystrix thresholds live, Kubernetes ConfigMaps and Secrets, HashiCorp Vault leasing credentials.

76
00:12:02.322 --> 00:12:06.222
The service template: Spotify’s Backstage and its golden path.

77
00:12:06.602 --> 00:12:21.045
The sidecar: Netflix’s Prana in 2014, then Envoy from Lyft in 2016, injected into every pod by Istio in 2017 — Burns and Oppenheimer named sidecar, ambassador, and adapter as the container patterns in 2016.

78
00:12:21.425 --> 00:12:29.413
The service mesh: Linkerd gave the category its name in February 2016; Istio paired Envoy with a control plane.

79
00:12:30.113 --> 00:12:37.985
The ambassador: Google’s Cloud SQL Auth Proxy — the app talks to localhost and never sees a certificate.

80
00:12:39.305 --> 00:12:42.637
Four hundred services cannot be stood up on a laptop.

81
00:12:43.017 --> 00:12:55.637
Consumer-driven contracts: Pact, written in 2013 for REA Group, whose Ruby services kept breaking each other; the Pact Broker’s ‘can I deploy’ check; Spring Cloud Contract with the artifact flowing the other way.

82
00:12:56.017 --> 00:13:02.007
Consumer-side tests against stubs derived from the contract — WireMock when there is no framework.

83
00:13:03.047 --> 00:13:17.235
And the service component test: Testcontainers starting a real Postgres or Kafka for one test; Shopify’s Toxiproxy injecting latency to ask what the service does when Redis is slow, not only when it is gone.

84
00:13:18.555 --> 00:13:22.700
Four teams own the product, the price, the reviews, and the recommendations.

85
00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:37.743
Server-side fragments: Facebook’s BigPipe, 2010, streaming pagelets as each is ready; Zalando’s Mosaic and Tailor for a company of many teams; Edge Side Includes assembling a page in the cache since 2001.

86
00:13:38.123 --> 00:13:54.029
Client-side composition: Spotify’s desktop client built from squad-owned web views; micro-frontends, on the Thoughtworks radar in 2016 and written up by Cam Jackson in 2019; single-spa; webpack’s Module Federation removing the iframe in 2020.

87
00:13:55.009 --> 00:14:00.071
Three observations travel forward from these sixty-one stops.

88
00:14:01.111 --> 00:14:11.815
First, the data patterns — saga, outbox, replica, CQRS — were designed by hand in every case, with the business rule and the compensation written by someone who knew the domain.

89
00:14:12.195 --> 00:14:22.690
Second, the reliability and discovery patterns have already moved once, from Netflix’s libraries into Envoy and the mesh — so ‘where does the pattern live’ is a question with precedent.

90
00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:32.168
Third, several companies moved back — Segment, Prime Video, Shopify never left — and each gave a cost as the reason.

91
00:14:33.208 --> 00:14:37.271
Any account of AI’s influence has to say which of these three it touched.

92
00:14:38.251 --> 00:14:40.306
Three things to carry away.

93
00:14:41.346 --> 00:14:41.915
One.

94
00:14:42.955 --> 00:14:46.473
Each pattern has a named case and a documented cost.

95
00:14:47.173 --> 00:14:47.660
Two.

96
00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:55.341
Data patterns were hand-designed everywhere; reliability patterns have already moved once, from libraries into the mesh.

97
00:14:55.721 --> 00:14:56.487
Three.

98
00:14:57.527 --> 00:15:01.568
Several companies moved back — and each gave a cost as the reason.

99
00:15:02.548 --> 00:15:05.021
Next: inside one service.

100
00:15:06.061 --> 00:15:10.136
Where does a service end, and who decides?

101
00:15:11.176 --> 00:15:14.717
Domain-driven design, with a delivery drone.
